ese dietary reference intakes</source><year>2000</year><publisher-name>Beijing, China: China Light Industry Publishing House</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gibson</surname><given-names>RS</given-names></name></person-group><source>Principles of Nutritional Assessment</source><year>2005</year><edition>Second</edition><publisher-name>New York: Oxford University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>Department of Health</collab></person-group><source>Report on Health and Social Subjects no 41</source><year>1991</year><publisher-name>Dietary Reference Values for Food, Energy and Nutrients for the United Kingdom. London</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>IOM</collab></person-group><source>Dietary Reference Intakes for Vitamin A, Vitamin K, Arsenic, Boron, Chromium, Copper, Iodine, Iron, Manganese, Molybdenum, Nickel, Silicon, Vaadium, and Zinc</source><year>2001</year><publisher-name>Washington, DC: National Academy Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gibson</surname><given-names>RS</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferguson</surname><given-names>EL</given-names></name></person-group><source>An interactive 24-hour recall for assessing the adequacy of iron and zinc intakes in developing countries</source><year>1999</year><publisher-name>Washington, DC: International Life Sciences Institutes Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B27"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>FAO/WHO</collab></person-group><source>Requirements of vitamin A, iron, folate and vitamin B12 Report of a Joint FAO/WHO Expert Consultation</source><year>1988</year>